1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,340
Type "The capital of France is" into a chatbot,
and it answers Paris in a fraction

2
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,100
of a second.

3
00:00:06,717 --> 00:00:08,867
It didn't look that up anywhere.

4
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:14,330
A file of numbers gave every possible next
piece of text a probability, and Paris

5
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:15,542
came out far ahead.

6
00:00:16,150 --> 00:00:19,925
So it picks Paris, appends it, and
runs the whole thing again.

7
00:00:20,225 --> 00:00:22,225
That loop is the whole trick.

8
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:27,576
By the end of this video, you'll know why the
same question can get different

9
00:00:27,576 --> 00:00:28,025
answers.

10
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:34,175
And why people preferred a model more than
100 times smaller than GPT-3.

11
00:00:37,183 --> 00:00:40,108
This is Part 1 of a 16-part series.

12
00:00:40,408 --> 00:00:44,233
Today is the big picture, from your
text to the next token.

13
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:50,276
Each later part opens up one piece of it:
tokenizers, attention, the Transformer

14
00:00:50,276 --> 00:00:53,600
block, and how a base model
becomes an assistant.

15
00:00:54,617 --> 00:00:57,317
A large language model does one thing.

16
00:00:57,617 --> 00:01:02,817
Given some text, it predicts which small piece
of text, called a token, comes next.

17
00:01:03,417 --> 00:01:06,567
Think of the world's most
well-read autocomplete.

18
00:01:07,183 --> 00:01:10,908
Your phone suggests the next word
from the last word or two.

19
00:01:11,208 --> 00:01:14,658
An LLM looks at everything
you've written so far.

20
00:01:15,683 --> 00:01:20,208
To continue a sentence about penicillin,
you need to know some medicine.

21
00:01:20,817 --> 00:01:24,742
To finish a half-written Python
function, you need to know Python.

22
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:30,471
So training a model to predict text well
forces it to pick up grammar, facts and

23
00:01:30,471 --> 00:01:32,775
patterns of reasoning along the way.

24
00:01:33,783 --> 00:01:35,583
Here's the whole machine.

25
00:01:35,883 --> 00:01:39,383
Let's follow our sentence through
it, one block at a time.

26
00:01:39,983 --> 00:01:43,783
It starts with the text so far, "The
capital of France is".

27
00:01:44,383 --> 00:01:49,587
First, a tokenizer splits the text into
tokens, small pieces from a fixed

28
00:01:49,587 --> 00:01:53,508
vocabulary, and replaces each
piece with its ID number.

29
00:01:54,117 --> 00:01:59,595
Next, each ID is swapped for a vector, a long
list of numbers, taken from a lookup

30
00:01:59,595 --> 00:02:01,867
table called the embedding matrix.

31
00:02:02,483 --> 00:02:07,932
Then a stack of identical Transformer blocks
updates each vector with information

32
00:02:07,932 --> 00:02:13,314
from the tokens before it, using attention and
a small neural network called the

33
00:02:13,314 --> 00:02:13,583
MLP.

34
00:02:14,183 --> 00:02:19,428
After the last block, the final vector becomes
one score for every token in the

35
00:02:19,428 --> 00:02:20,158
vocabulary.

36
00:02:20,458 --> 00:02:22,583
These scores are called logits.

37
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:27,408
Softmax turns those scores into
probabilities that add up to 1.

38
00:02:28,017 --> 00:02:31,292
Then one token is picked from
those probabilities.

39
00:02:31,592 --> 00:02:33,342
Here, it's Paris.

40
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:38,951
Paris is appended to the text, and the whole
model runs again to predict the token

41
00:02:38,951 --> 00:02:39,500
after it.

42
00:02:40,117 --> 00:02:43,242
One trip through all of this
produces one token.

43
00:02:43,542 --> 00:02:45,917
Now let's take each block in turn.

44
00:02:46,917 --> 00:02:49,517
A model can only work with numbers.

45
00:02:49,817 --> 00:02:55,092
So first, a tokenizer splits text into
pieces from a fixed vocabulary.

46
00:02:55,717 --> 00:02:59,767
Here's our sentence as GPT-4's
tokenizer splits it.

47
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:03,442
Five pieces, and the spaces are
part of the pieces.

48
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:09,700
Then each piece is replaced by its ID
number. France is token 9822.

49
00:03:10,717 --> 00:03:15,992
In English, one token is about 4 characters,
roughly three quarters of a word.

50
00:03:16,617 --> 00:03:18,592
Vocabularies are big.

51
00:03:18,892 --> 00:03:23,617
GPT-2 used 50,257 tokens.

52
00:03:24,217 --> 00:03:27,717
Llama 3 uses 128,000.

53
00:03:28,017 --> 00:03:32,392
Tokens are also how you're billed, and how
context limits are measured.

54
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:37,617
A token ID like 9822 is just a label.

55
00:03:37,917 --> 00:03:44,317
Token 9822 isn't bigger than token
315 in any useful sense.

56
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:50,257
So each ID is swapped for a vector, a list of
numbers, from a lookup table called

57
00:03:50,257 --> 00:03:51,642
the embedding matrix.

58
00:03:51,942 --> 00:03:56,567
In GPT-3, that's 12,288 numbers.

59
00:03:57,183 --> 00:04:02,548
During training, related meanings end up near
each other: France, Paris and French

60
00:04:02,548 --> 00:04:05,033
sit closer than France and carburetor.

61
00:04:05,333 --> 00:04:07,183
Nobody designs that.

62
00:04:07,483 --> 00:04:09,208
The model learns it.

63
00:04:10,217 --> 00:04:13,942
A single token's vector only
knows about that token.

64
00:04:14,242 --> 00:04:19,042
The word bank gets the same starting vector in
"river bank" and "bank account".

65
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:24,674
So the model updates each vector with
information from the tokens around it, in a

66
00:04:24,674 --> 00:04:27,900
stack of identical layers called
Transformer blocks.

67
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:31,250
GPT-3 has 96 of them.

68
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:36,400
Llama 3.1 405B has 126.

69
00:04:37,417 --> 00:04:39,242
First, attention.

70
00:04:39,542 --> 00:04:44,642
Every token looks back at the tokens before it
and decides which matter right now.

71
00:04:44,942 --> 00:04:48,692
The position for "is" weights
capital and France heavily.

72
00:04:49,317 --> 00:04:53,267
Second, a feed-forward network,
also called the MLP.

73
00:04:53,567 --> 00:04:57,690
It works on each token on its own, and it's
where much of the model's stored

74
00:04:57,690 --> 00:04:58,992
knowledge seems to live.

75
00:04:59,617 --> 00:05:03,442
Each block adds what it computed
instead of replacing it.

76
00:05:03,742 --> 00:05:09,477
Picture a shared notepad per token that each of
the 96 layers reads and writes a few

77
00:05:09,477 --> 00:05:10,092
notes on.

78
00:05:11,117 --> 00:05:12,267
Wait.

79
00:05:12,567 --> 00:05:16,367
So each token only sees the
words right next to it?

80
00:05:16,983 --> 00:05:18,208
No.

81
00:05:18,508 --> 00:05:23,600
Attention connects every token to every
earlier token directly, in one step, no

82
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,533
matter how far apart they are.

83
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:31,795
After the last block, the final vector becomes
one score for every token in the

84
00:05:31,795 --> 00:05:32,525
vocabulary.

85
00:05:32,825 --> 00:05:35,225
These raw scores are called logits.

86
00:05:35,525 --> 00:05:37,575
Here are five made-up ones.

87
00:05:38,183 --> 00:05:40,508
Scores aren't probabilities yet.

88
00:05:40,808 --> 00:05:43,633
A function called softmax converts them.

89
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:49,310
It raises e to the power of each score, then
divides by the total, so everything

90
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:50,183
adds up to 1.

91
00:05:51,183 --> 00:05:52,833
Here's a question.

92
00:05:53,133 --> 00:05:56,283
Paris leads "a" by 2.5 points.

93
00:05:56,583 --> 00:05:59,283
How big is its lead after softmax?

94
00:06:04,283 --> 00:06:05,883
Twelve to one.

95
00:06:06,183 --> 00:06:10,958
Exponentials turn a small lead in score
into a big lead in probability.

96
00:06:12,783 --> 00:06:15,608
Paris gets 85.2%.

97
00:06:15,908 --> 00:06:19,733
Then a with 7%, and the rest
trail far behind.

98
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:24,050
That step is called sampling,
and you control it.

99
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:27,500
Greedy decoding always takes
the top token.

100
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:30,675
It's predictable, but tends to sound flat.

101
00:06:31,283 --> 00:06:38,383
Sampling rolls weighted dice: Paris 85.2%
of the time, "a" 7% of the time.

102
00:06:38,683 --> 00:06:41,933
That's why the same prompt can
give different answers.

103
00:06:42,550 --> 00:06:47,388
And temperature reshapes the odds before the
roll, by dividing every logit by a

104
00:06:47,388 --> 00:06:48,000
number, T.

105
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:51,767
Here are our five candidates at
a temperature of 1.

106
00:06:52,383 --> 00:06:54,833
Below 1, the leader gets stronger.

107
00:06:55,133 --> 00:06:57,733
Paris goes to 99%.

108
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:00,450
Above 1, the field flattens.

109
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:06,425
At a temperature of 2, Lyon goes from almost
never to about one draw in seventeen.

110
00:07:06,725 --> 00:07:10,475
That's the creativity knob, and
also a source of mistakes.

111
00:07:11,483 --> 00:07:12,958
Another one.

112
00:07:13,258 --> 00:07:18,383
To write a 500-token answer, how many trips
through the whole model does it take?

113
00:07:23,383 --> 00:07:24,933
500.

114
00:07:25,233 --> 00:07:30,708
One trip produces one token, and every
trip runs through all 96 layers.

115
00:07:31,008 --> 00:07:34,833
That fact explains a lot about
LLM speed and cost.

116
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:40,672
Once a token is chosen, it's appended, and
the whole model runs again on "The

117
00:07:40,672 --> 00:07:42,425
capital of France is Paris".

118
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:48,342
The loop keeps going until the model produces a
special end token, or hits a length

119
00:07:48,342 --> 00:07:48,725
limit.

120
00:07:49,750 --> 00:07:53,800
Every step you've seen uses learned
numbers, called parameters.

121
00:07:54,100 --> 00:07:56,400
Autocomplete stores phrases.

122
00:07:56,700 --> 00:07:59,800
An LLM stores billions of numbers.

123
00:08:00,417 --> 00:08:05,004
That's why it can't tell you where it learned
something, and why it sometimes fills

124
00:08:05,004 --> 00:08:06,717
gaps with confident inventions.

125
00:08:07,717 --> 00:08:10,817
You can rebuild GPT-3's count yourself.

126
00:08:11,117 --> 00:08:14,792
Each block holds about 12 times d
squared parameters.

127
00:08:15,092 --> 00:08:20,542
With d at 12,288, that's 1.81
billion per block.

128
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:25,925
Times 96 blocks gives 173.9 billion.

129
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:32,494
Add 0.6 billion for the embeddings, and you're
within half a percent of the 175

130
00:08:32,494 --> 00:08:34,450
billion the paper reports.

131
00:08:35,450 --> 00:08:38,525
Each parameter is usually
stored in 2 bytes.

132
00:08:38,825 --> 00:08:43,875
So GPT-3's weights alone
take about 350 GB.

133
00:08:44,483 --> 00:08:48,033
That's more than four 80 GB GPUs can hold.

134
00:08:48,333 --> 00:08:51,533
And every generated token
touches all of them.

135
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:55,125
None of these numbers is set by hand.

136
00:08:55,425 --> 00:08:57,125
They start random.

137
00:08:57,425 --> 00:08:59,425
Training takes real text.

138
00:09:00,050 --> 00:09:03,750
At every position, the model
predicts the next token.

139
00:09:04,350 --> 00:09:09,175
The loss measures how surprised it was by
the token that actually came next.

140
00:09:09,783 --> 00:09:15,160
Backpropagation works out how to nudge every
parameter so the right answer gets more

141
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:15,608
likely.

142
00:09:16,217 --> 00:09:18,467
Repeat, trillions of times.

143
00:09:18,767 --> 00:09:20,767
No one labels anything.

144
00:09:21,067 --> 00:09:23,417
The text is its own answer key.

145
00:09:24,417 --> 00:09:28,217
GPT-3 trained on 300 billion tokens.

146
00:09:28,817 --> 00:09:34,317
Llama 3.1 405B trained on 15.6 trillion.

147
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:42,392
Its 30.84 million GPU-hours would take
about 3,500 years on a single GPU.

148
00:09:43,417 --> 00:09:47,042
A model trained only on web
text is a base model.

149
00:09:47,342 --> 00:09:52,249
Ask it a question, and it might reply with
three more questions, like a web page

150
00:09:52,249 --> 00:09:52,617
would.

151
00:09:53,217 --> 00:09:56,417
So chat models get a second
round of training.

152
00:09:56,717 --> 00:10:03,630
In the InstructGPT paper, people preferred a 1.3
billion parameter instruction-tuned

153
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:07,992
model over GPT-3, a model more
than 100 times bigger.

154
00:10:09,017 --> 00:10:14,862
Before 2017, the best language models read one
word at a time, carrying a running

155
00:10:14,862 --> 00:10:16,017
summary forward.

156
00:10:16,317 --> 00:10:20,617
Early words faded, and the work
couldn't be spread across a GPU.

157
00:10:21,217 --> 00:10:24,717
The Transformer paper named the
problem directly.

158
00:10:25,017 --> 00:10:28,192
Attention processes every
position at once.

159
00:10:29,217 --> 00:10:32,817
It set a new best score on English
to German translation.

160
00:10:33,417 --> 00:10:36,867
Its base model trained in 12
hours on one machine.

161
00:10:37,167 --> 00:10:39,642
Then the models mostly got bigger.

162
00:10:40,650 --> 00:10:47,922
From GPT-1's 117 million to Kimi K3's 2.8
trillion, parameter counts grew about

163
00:10:47,922 --> 00:10:50,500
24,000 times in eight years.

164
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:53,675
And the block design
underneath barely changed.

165
00:10:54,683 --> 00:10:59,885
The one big change is mixture of experts:
many smaller MLPs per block, called

166
00:10:59,885 --> 00:11:03,533
experts, and a router that picks
a few for each token.

167
00:11:04,150 --> 00:11:11,918
Kimi K3 uses 16 of its 896 experts per token,
so only about 104 billion of its 2.8

168
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:14,950
trillion parameters do any work.

169
00:11:15,250 --> 00:11:19,025
A giant's knowledge, at a much
smaller model's compute cost.

170
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:22,900
So it looks answers up in a database?

171
00:11:23,517 --> 00:11:25,717
There's no database inside.

172
00:11:26,017 --> 00:11:29,017
Facts are spread across
billions of parameters.

173
00:11:29,317 --> 00:11:32,567
That's why models can be wrong
with full confidence.

174
00:11:33,183 --> 00:11:34,533
Fine.

175
00:11:34,833 --> 00:11:38,483
But temperature zero makes it
deterministic, right?

176
00:11:39,083 --> 00:11:40,958
It makes the choice greedy.

177
00:11:41,258 --> 00:11:45,846
But on a shared server, your request is
batched with other people's, and the

178
00:11:45,846 --> 00:11:47,958
arithmetic can shift very slightly.

179
00:11:48,258 --> 00:11:50,333
Enough to flip a close call.

180
00:11:50,950 --> 00:11:53,625
And it reads words, the way I do.

181
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:56,025
It reads tokens.

182
00:11:56,325 --> 00:12:00,725
That's why counting letters can trip up a
model that writes fluent essays.

183
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:03,550
So, the big picture.

184
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:09,050
An LLM predicts one token at a time:
predict, pick, append, repeat.

185
00:12:09,650 --> 00:12:15,425
Each trip turns tokens into vectors, mixes in
context, and ends in probabilities.

186
00:12:15,725 --> 00:12:18,800
Temperature sets how
adventurous the pick is.

187
00:12:19,417 --> 00:12:24,216
And all of the model's skill lives in its
parameters, learned from trillions of

188
00:12:24,216 --> 00:12:24,642
tokens.

189
00:12:25,650 --> 00:12:31,027
Next is Part 2, the first thing that happens
to your text: tokenization, and why

190
00:12:31,027 --> 00:12:33,850
"strawberry" is hard for a model to spell.

191
00:12:34,850 --> 00:12:39,075
The full article, with every number and
its source, is on vidyal.ai.

192
00:12:40,083 --> 00:12:41,783
Thanks for watching.

193
00:12:42,083 --> 00:12:44,733
Next in the series, tokenization.

194
00:12:45,033 --> 00:12:47,108
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
